// Lint waivers for the Domesday Duplicator gateware.
//
// NB: in a .vlt file a comment whose first word is the linter's own name is
// parsed as a pragma, not as a comment, so it must not start a line here.
//
// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2018-2026 Simon Inns
// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
//
// The lint check (T4) runs with -Wall, which is the only setting that catches
// anything interesting in a design this small. -Wall also reports a fixed set
// of things that are correct here, and this file is where each of those is
// waived *with the reason*. A waiver with no reason is indistinguishable from
// a bug someone silenced.
//
// Two rules for editing this file:
//
//   1. Waivers are matched on rule, file and message text, never on line
//      number, so they survive edits to the source above them.
//   2. Every waiver is scoped as narrowly as the match syntax allows. A
//      blanket `lint_off -rule X` would hide the next occurrence of X in code
//      that has not been looked at, which is the failure mode this whole check
//      exists to avoid.
//
// The waivers in section 2 describe real properties of source that has been in
// the field since 2018. They are candidates for a gateware cleanup, but that
// belongs in a change whose gate is a capture-integrity run on hardware — not
// in a change that adds a linter.

`verilator_config

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. The Altera black box
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// IPpllGenerator_bb.v is a port declaration with no body — that is what a black
// box is. The linter is right that nothing drives the outputs and nothing reads
// the inputs, and it would be right about any black box. It is linted rather
// than excluded because the top level cannot elaborate without it, and linting
// the module that instantiates the IP is the point.
//
// The real implementation, IPpllGenerator.v, instantiates altpll from Altera's
// altera_mf library, which has no free simulation model, so it is not linted at
// all — see fpga/README.md.
//
// There were two of these until fifo.v replaced the dcfifo the buffer was built
// on. One black box is left, so these are scoped to it by name rather than to
// any file ending in _bb.v.

lint_off -rule DECLFILENAME -file "*IPpllGenerator_bb.v"
lint_off -rule UNUSEDSIGNAL -file "*IPpllGenerator_bb.v"
lint_off -rule UNDRIVEN     -file "*IPpllGenerator_bb.v"

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. Project source
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// dataGenerator.v: `if (sequence_count == (6'd63 << 16) - 1)`.
//
// The linter objects that the 6-bit constant is widened by its context before
// the shift. It is, and that is what makes the expression correct: the
// comparison is against the 22-bit sequence_count, so 63 is widened to 22 bits
// and 63 << 16 is 4128768, which fits. Had the shift been evaluated at the
// constant's own width the result would be zero and the sequence number would
// never advance.
//
// This is the one implicit width promotion in the design that decides
// behaviour rather than merely being tidy, so it is pinned by simulation
// instead of being left to a reading of the standard:
// tests/tb_dataGenerator.v asserts that the counter wraps to 0 after exactly
// 63 sequence numbers of 65536 samples each.
lint_off -rule WIDTHEXPAND -file "*/dataGenerator.v" -match "*SHIFTL*"

// fx3StateMachine.v: `case (sm_current_state)` covers 2 of 16 states.
//
// The case is preceded by `sm_next_state = sm_current_state;`, so every
// uncovered state holds rather than falling through to an undefined value, and
// the register can only ever hold the two named states.
lint_off -rule CASEINCOMPLETE -file "*/fx3StateMachine.v"

// fx3StateMachine.v: word_counter is written with blocking assignments inside a
// clocked block.
//
// A genuine style defect, and the one waiver here that is not merely cosmetic:
// blocking assignment in sequential logic can make simulation disagree with
// synthesis. It does not here, because the combinational next-state block is
// `always @(*)` and re-evaluates when word_counter settles, so the value latched
// at the following edge is the same either way.
//
// "Does not here" is a claim about behaviour, so it is tested rather than
// asserted: tests/tb_fx3StateMachine.v measures the packet length and requires
// exactly 8192 clock cycles. A packet one word short is invisible in a capture
// and corrupts every sample after it.
lint_off -rule BLKSEQ -file "*/fx3StateMachine.v"

// DomesdayDuplicator.v: the reset synchroniser is flopped both ways.
//
// Two findings, both describing the same three registers, and both correct:
// a reset synchroniser is asynchronously reset *and* clocked, because that is
// what it is for. fx3_reset_n arrives from the FX3 with no relationship to the
// system clock, so it has to assert asynchronously - a reset that needed a
// clock edge would not work when there is no clock. Releasing it on an edge is
// the half that has to be synchronised, and the only way to write that is a
// shift register whose own flops are asynchronously reset.
//
// SYNCASYNCNET fires on every correct implementation of this structure. It is
// worth having on for the case it is really looking for - a signal used as a
// reset in one place and as data in another by accident - so it is waived
// here by the names of the two nets involved rather than for the file.
//
// GPIO1 is the same finding seen one level further back: fx3_reset_n is
// GPIO1[07] through the control-bus mapping, and other bits of the same bus
// carry the SPI lines and read_data, which are ordinary synchronous inputs.
// The linter reasons about the bus, not the bit. The design cannot separate
// them because the pin assignments are fixed by the PCB.
lint_off -rule SYNCASYNCNET -file "*/DomesdayDuplicator.v" -match "*reset_n_sync*"
lint_off -rule SYNCASYNCNET -file "*/DomesdayDuplicator.v" -match "*GPIO1*"

// DomesdayDuplicator.v: bits 2 and 6-9 of fx3_control are unused.
//
// These are the FX3 CTL lines the board wires up but the design does not use —
// CTL_02 (collectData) and CTL_06 to CTL_09 (configuration bits 1 to 4). The
// source names each one as unused in the signal map. They stay in the bus
// because the pin assignments are fixed by the PCB.
lint_off -rule UNUSEDSIGNAL -file "*/DomesdayDuplicator.v" -match "*fx3_control*"
